<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Two ships are on the equator at <m>12^\circ\,\mathrm{E}</m> and <m>47^\circ\,\mathrm{E}</m>. Take <m>\pi = \tfrac{22}{7}</m> and <m>R = 6370\,\text{km}</m>. Distances in km within 0.1% of the exact value are accepted.</p><p>(a) Find the shorter distance between them along the equator, in nautical miles.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/> nm  </p><p>(b) Find this distance in kilometres, to the nearest km.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/> km  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Both ships are on the same side of the Greenwich meridian, so subtract the longitudes. <m>|{12}^\circ - {47}^\circ| = {35}^\circ</m>. The equator is a great circle, so the distance in nm is the angle in minutes: <m>{35} \times 60 = {2100}</m> nm.</p><p>(b) <me>\frac{ {35} }{360} \times 2 \times \frac{22}{7} \times 6370 = \frac{ {35} }{360} \times 40\,040 = {3892.8} \approx {3893}\,\text{km}.</me></p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>2100</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>3892.77777778</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>